<!-- Debrief (shown after completion) -->
<div class="lab-debrief" id="lab-debrief">
  <div class="lab-debrief-label">Debrief — Insecure Direct Object Reference</div>

  <h3>What happened</h3>
  <p>MedConnect's patient API authenticates users (checks that you're logged in) but never authorizes individual requests (never checks that you're allowed to see patient 1002 vs. 1001). Any authenticated user — physician, nurse, billing clerk — can access any patient record by changing the ID in the URL.</p>

  <h3>The vulnerable code</h3>
  <pre>router.get('/api/patient/:id', (req, res) =&gt; {
  const patient = db.getPatient(req.params.id);
  // No check: does req.user have access to req.params.id?
  return res.json(patient);
});</pre>

  <h3>The fix</h3>
  <p>Every data access must verify that the authenticated user is authorized for that specific resource. This means checking a mapping table (physician_assignments, care_team, etc.) before returning data. Additionally:</p>
  <pre>// 1. Check authorization
const allowed = db.getAssignedPatients(req.user.id);
if (!allowed.includes(req.params.id)) {
  return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Access denied' });
}

// 2. Use UUIDs instead of sequential integers
// Bad:  /api/patient/1001 (guessable)
// Good: /api/patient/a3f8d2e1-4c91-4b... (not guessable)

// 3. Log access attempts for audit trail
audit.log({
  user: req.user.id,
  action: 'patient_access',
  resource: req.params.id,
  authorized: allowed.includes(req.params.id)
});</pre>

  <h3>Real-world impact</h3>
  <p>In healthcare, IDOR vulnerabilities are a HIPAA violation that can result in fines of $100–$50,000 per record accessed, up to $1.5 million per year. Beyond financial penalties, exposed patient records erode trust and can be used for medical identity theft.</p>
  <p><strong>First American Financial (2019):</strong> A simple IDOR vulnerability in the document access system exposed 885 million records — including bank account numbers, Social Security numbers, and wire transaction receipts — dating back to 2003. Anyone who knew the URL format could access any document by incrementing the ID number. The SEC later fined First American $487,616.</p>
  <p><strong>LabCorp / Quest Diagnostics (2019):</strong> AMCA, a billing collections vendor, had an IDOR vulnerability that exposed 20 million patient records including lab results and financial data.</p>

  <h3>How to find this in the wild</h3>
  <p>During a pentest, identify any endpoint that uses an ID, filename, or key in the URL or request body. Create two accounts with different permissions. Using Account A's session, try to access Account B's resources by changing the identifier. Also test:</p>
  <p><strong>Horizontal IDOR:</strong> Same role, different data (patient 1001 vs 1002)<br>
  <strong>Vertical IDOR:</strong> Different role, higher-privilege data (admin panel, VIP records)<br>
  <strong>Parameter pollution:</strong> <code>?fields=all</code>, <code>?include=ssn</code>, <code>?verbose=true</code></p>

  <div class="lab-debrief-refs">
    <div class="lab-section-label">References</div>
